Here's an example of a Depth-First Search (DFS) algorithm implemented in C++ using std::vector:

```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <stack>

using namespace std;

// Function to perform DFS traversal
vector<int> dfs(vector<vector<int>>& graph, int start) {
    vector<bool> visited(graph.size(), false); // keep track of visited nodes
    vector<int> result; // vertices visited in DFS order
    stack<int> st; // stack for DFS traversal
    st.push(start);

    while(!st.empty()) {
        int vertex = st.top();
        st.pop();

        if(!visited[vertex]) {
            visited[vertex] = true;
            result.push_back(vertex);

            // push adjacent vertices to the top of the stack
            for(int i=graph[vertex].size()-1; i>=0; i--) {
                if(!visited[graph[vertex][i]]) {
                    st.push(graph[vertex][i]);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return result;
}

int main() {
    // Example graph
    vector<vector<int>> graph = {{1,2},{0,2,3},{0,1,3},{1,2}};
    vector<int> result = dfs(graph, 0);

    // Output the vertices visited in DFS order
    for(int i=0; i<result.size(); i++) {
        cout << result[i] << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code defines a `dfs` function that takes in a graph represented as an adjacency list using `std::vector`. The function performs DFS traversal of the graph starting from a given `start` vertex. It returns a `std::vector` containing the vertices visited in DFS order.

The DFS algorithm is implemented using a stack. Initially, the start vertex is pushed onto the stack. Then, while the stack is not empty, the top vertex is popped off the stack. If the vertex has not been visited yet, it is marked as visited and added to the result vector. Next, all adjacent vertices that have not been visited yet are pushed onto the stack. The order of adjacent vertices pushed onto the stack determines the order of traversal.

In `main`, an example graph is defined as a `std::vector` of `std::vector`s of integers. The `dfs` function is called with the graph and a starting vertex of 0. The resulting vector of visited vertices is output using `std::cout`.

